19041:
Calculate the magnitude of the net resultant force:
The two forces F_1 = 8\text{ N} and F_2 = 6\text{ N} are mutually perpendicular (\theta = 90^\circ):
F_{\text{net}} = \sqrt{F_1^2 + F_2^2} = \sqrt{8^2 + 6^2} = \sqrt{64 + 36} = \sqrt{100} = 10\text{ N}

Calculate the magnitude of acceleration:
Given mass m = 5\text{ kg}:
a = \frac{F_{\text{net}}}{m} = \frac{10\text{ N}}{5\text{ kg}} = 2\text{ m s}^{-2}

Determine the direction of the acceleration:
The direction of acceleration aligns with the resultant force. The angle \alpha made by the resultant force with the 8\text{ N} force is:
\tan \alpha = \frac{F_2}{F_1} = \frac{6}{8} = \frac{3}{4} \implies \alpha = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{3}{4}\right) \text{ with the } 8\text{ N force}
→ (D)

19042:
Calculate the initial electrostatic energy stored in the charged capacitor:
Capacitance: C_1 = 200\text{ pF} = 200 \times 10^{-12}\text{ F}
Voltage: V_1 = 100\text{ V}
Second capacitor: C_2 = 200\text{ pF}, initially uncharged (V_2 = 0\text{ V})

Use the standard formula for electrostatic energy lost when two capacitors are connected in parallel:
\Delta U = \frac{1}{2} \frac{C_1 C_2}{C_1 + C_2} (V_1 - V_2)^2

Since C_1 = C_2 = C = 200\text{ pF}:
\frac{C_1 C_2}{C_1 + C_2} = \frac{C}{2} = 100\text{ pF} = 10^{-10}\text{ F}

Substitute the values:
\Delta U = \frac{1}{2} \times (10^{-10}\text{ F}) \times (100 - 0)^2
\Delta U = \frac{1}{2} \times 10^{-10} \times 10^4 = \frac{1}{2} \times 10^{-6}\text{ J} = 0.5 \times 10^{-6}\text{ J}
→ (A)

